            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### 1. What Does \"Redox\" Even Mean?",
            "start": 389,
            "end": 424
          },
          {
            "id": "p7",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "The word **Redox** is a portmanteau (a blend of two words):",
            "start": 426,
            "end": 485
          },
          {
            "id": "p8",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **Red** = **Reduction**",
            "start": 486,
            "end": 513
          },
          {
            "id": "p9",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "*   **Ox** = **Oxidation**",
            "start": 514,
            "end": 540
          },
          {
            "id": "p10",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Here is the golden rule of redox: **They are a package deal.** You cannot have oxidation without reduction, and you cannot have reduction without oxidation. ",
            "start": 542,
            "end": 699
          },
          {
            "id": "p11",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Think of it like a game of catch. One person cannot *catch* a ball unless another person *throws* it. In chemistry, the \"ball\" is an **electron ($e^-$)**.",
            "start": 701,
            "end": 855
          },
          {
            "id": "p12",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 857,
            "end": 860
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "Accurately defines redox as the pairing of reduction and oxidation and correctly explains that electron transfer requires one species to lose electrons while another gains them.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
