            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### 3. Why is \"Gaining\" called \"Reduction\"? (The Big Confusion)",
            "start": 1284,
            "end": 1347
          },
          {
            "id": "p21",
            "category": "CAVEAT",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "misconception"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Students often ask: *\"Teacher, if I am gaining something, shouldn't that be called an increase? Why is it called a reduction?\"*",
            "start": 1349,
            "end": 1476
          },
          {
            "id": "p22",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Remember: **Electrons have a negative charge ($-1$).**",
            "start": 1478,
            "end": 1532
          },
          {
            "id": "p23",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "If you gain negative charges, your overall charge goes down (it is *reduced*).",
            "start": 1533,
            "end": 1611
          },
          {
            "id": "p24",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* If you start at $0$ and gain an electron, your charge is now $-1$. Your charge was **reduced**.",
            "start": 1612,
            "end": 1709
          },
          {
            "id": "p25",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 1711,
            "end": 1714
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "Accurately resolves the confusion by stating that electrons carry a negative charge (-1), which reduces the overall oxidation state or electrical charge upon gain.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
