            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### 4. Let’s Look at an Example: Making Table Salt",
            "start": 1716,
            "end": 1766
          },
          {
            "id": "p27",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Let’s look at how Sodium ($\\text{Na}$) and Chlorine ($\\text{Cl}$) react to make standard table salt ($\\text{NaCl}$):",
            "start": 1768,
            "end": 1884
          },
          {
            "id": "p28",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$\\text{Na} + \\text{Cl} \\rightarrow \\text{Na}^+ + \\text{Cl}^-$$",
            "start": 1886,
            "end": 1949
          },
          {
            "id": "p29",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "1.  **Sodium ($\\text{Na}$):** It starts neutral ($0$). It gives away one electron to become $\\text{Na}^+$. ",
            "start": 1951,
            "end": 2058
          },
          {
            "id": "p30",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    *   It **lost** an electron. ",
            "start": 2059,
            "end": 2092
          },
          {
            "id": "p31",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    *   According to OIL RIG, Sodium was **oxidized**.",
            "start": 2093,
            "end": 2147
          },
          {
            "id": "p32",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "2.  **Chlorine ($\\text{Cl}$):** It starts neutral ($0$). It takes that electron to become $\\text{Cl}^-$.",
            "start": 2148,
            "end": 2252
          },
          {
            "id": "p33",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    *   It **gained** an electron. ",
            "start": 2253,
            "end": 2288
          },
          {
            "id": "p34",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "    *   According to OIL RIG, Chlorine was **reduced**.",
            "start": 2289,
            "end": 2344
          },
          {
            "id": "p35",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 2346,
            "end": 2349
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "Correctly tracks electron transfer at an introductory atomic level: neutral sodium loses one electron to become Na+ (oxidation) and chlorine gains one electron to become Cl- (reduction).",
          "errors": []
        }
